# Retinal Irradiation & Weight Tuning

Identical `font-weight: 500` copy appears noticeably heavier in dark mode than in light mode, eroding typographic refinement.

## Optical Irradiation (The Helmholtz Illusion)

In 1867, physiological optics pioneer Hermann von Helmholtz documented this retinal mechanism in the *Handbuch der physiologischen Optik*:
**Photoreceptors stimulated by bright light spill excitation into neighboring dark-adapted cells, causing the visual cortex to perceive bright shapes expanding outward**.

## The Perceived Expansion of White Text

- **Light Mode (Dark on Light)**: Expansive light backgrounds encroach upon the boundaries of dark strokes, making typography feel compact and refined.
- **Dark Mode (Light on Dark)**: Radiant white strokes bleed into the surrounding darkness. **The same weight reads bolder than it does dark-on-light; how much depends on luminance, contrast and size, and there is no fixed percentage**.

Reusing identical numeric weights across themes causes dark mode typography to read clumsy and congested.

## Dark Theme 460/560/660 Tuning

TuffEx's dark theme takes each of its three emphasis weights 40 lighter: `--tx-font-weight-medium` goes from `500` to `460`, `--tx-font-weight-semibold` from `600` to `560`, and `--tx-font-weight-bold` from `700` to `660`. Components read them for emphasis, headings and figures, as `var(--tx-font-weight-medium, 500)` and the like, so they lighten when the dark theme applies; body text stays at `400`.

| Role | Weight Token | Light Weight | Dark Compensated Weight | Optical Target |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Body Default** | None | `400` (Regular) | `400` | Stays Regular: any lower and a static font falls to its Light face (see below) |
| **Inline Emphasis** | `--tx-font-weight-medium` | `500` (Medium) | `460` | Crisp emphasis without interior stroke collision |
| **Headings / Buttons** | `--tx-font-weight-semibold` | `600` (Semi-Bold) | `560` | Preserves letter counters and structural posture |
| **Bold / Figures** | `--tx-font-weight-bold` | `700` (Bold) | `660` | Keeps bold text and figures from swelling on dark |

These in-between values draw thinner only where the font has a weight axis: variable fonts (such as San Francisco, Segoe UI Variable or Inter Variable) draw `460` as it is; static fonts return to the light theme's face by the rules in the next section.

## Discrete Static Font Fallback Guards

A static font has a few fixed faces (PingFang SC and Microsoft YaHei both do), and CSS font matching assigns an in-between value to one of them:
- A target between `400` and `500`: search upward as far as `500`, then downward, and only then above `500`;
- A target above `500`: search upward, then downward;
- A target below `400`: search downward, then upward.

So a static font draws `460` with the `500` face, `560` with the `600` face and `660` with the `700` face (or, where that face is missing, the next one in the same order): the same face as the light theme. The compensation does nothing there, but it does not thicken the text either. TuffEx picks its dark weights on exactly this basis: each dark value lies within (light − 100, light]. The Sass function `tx-dark-weight($light, $dark)` checks this at build time, and a value outside the range, or any attempt to lower `400`, fails the build.

**The trap lies outside that range**:
- Body text at `390`: the target is below `400`, so a static font searches downward and draws the Light face, a whole step thinner. Measured in Chromium on macOS: with the family named `PingFang SC`, `380` and `390` both draw `PingFangSC-Light`, while the Chinese font that `system-ui` falls back to draws `390` as Regular. The same value gives different results depending on how the stack is written, so TuffEx leaves body text alone.
- `500` lowered to `400`: Medium on light becomes Regular on dark, again a whole step on a static font.

Where the font is static and weights cannot compensate, keep `400/500/600` and dial back text contrast instead (`--tx-text-color-regular` rather than raw white) to offset the expansion.

## What the Default Stack Shows

TuffEx's default stack, `--tx-font-family`, is `"Inter", "PingFang SC", "Microsoft YaHei", …`. On macOS without Inter installed, PingFang SC draws both Latin and Chinese. Measured, `460`, `560` and `660` draw Medium, Semibold and Semibold, exactly like `500`, `600` and `700` (PingFang SC has no Bold face, so `700` draws Semibold too), and the dark tuning does not show with the default stack. PingFang, which draws Chinese on macOS, has no weight axis, whether named directly or reached through `system-ui`; only a Latin font with an axis gets thinner. If a host sets `--tx-font-family` to a stack that starts with `system-ui`, for example, variable San Francisco draws the Latin text and `460` lands between `400` and `500`. The Nexus docs site sets its body text in PingFang SC as well; the demo below sets its Latin text in the `system-ui` stack, so the two outcomes sit side by side.

